  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $55%$ are produced by machine $B.$ If $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then, ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $55%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=0.55.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.08,\\,P(D|{A}^{\u2032})=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.55) \\\\  & =0.058.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
